    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d960236_47113d89",
        "filename": "security/keymint/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 224,
      "author": {
        "id": 1833664
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-06T17:28:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Based on discussions in the team chat, I think it would be better to check if the HAL supports backward compatibility instead.\n\n@trong@google.com helpfully pointed out https://source.corp.google.com/android-internal/hardware/interfaces/security/keymint/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp;l\u003d400\n\nWe just check to see if v1 support has been removed, if so, then CURVE_NONE makes sense. Else, the HAL supports v1 and we should ensure valid curves are returned.",
      "revId": "3730df0e0d2f0cb2e4b27199615abfdff16b9701",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5cca3fb6_8b13c57c",
        "filename": "security/keymint/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 224,
      "author": {
        "id": 1678423
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-06T21:17:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks Seth! I will try to add that check here.\n\nI would like to clarify a few things in the meanwhile:\n1) In the VTS tests, do we not test a particular instance of the IRPC HAL implementation?\nA particular instance can declare only one value for the version field in hwInfo (v2 or v3). Isn\u0027t it?\n2) It is not clear to me why we do not decide which set of VTS tests to run based on hwInfo.version, regardless of whether a particular instance previously supported the previous version or not.\n\nIn the check pointed out by @trong@google.com, there are two checks:\na) `rpcHardwareInfo.versionNumber \u003e\u003d VERSION_WITHOUT_TEST_MODE`\nb) `generateCertificateRequest` returns `STATUS_REMOVED`\nIt is not clear to me why b should not always be true if a is true. (i.e. how can a particular instance of a HAL implementation supports two versions at the same time)\n\nI would appreciate any clarifications. Thanks!",
      "parentUuid": "4d960236_47113d89",
      "revId": "3730df0e0d2f0cb2e4b27199615abfdff16b9701",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "35c0e410_2cfd477f",
        "filename": "security/keymint/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 224,
      "author": {
        "id": 1833664
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-06T21:49:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e A particular instance can declare only one value for the version field in hwInfo (v2 or v3).\n\nTrue, but Tri had a neat idea to allow v3 implementations to support v2 clients. So we cannot rely only on the hwInfo version to determine the curve support.\n\nThe confusing bit here, I think, is that there are two different versions:\n1. The HAL version\n2. The CSR version (the on-the-wire format that the HAL spits out)\n\nWith the changes Tri is working on now, we\u0027re slightly decoupling this, so a newer HAL can support older CSR versions. There are different entry points for getting the different CSR versions, so we can figure out which one the client wants.\n\nWe need the P256 and x25519 curves to be supported in HALs that support the old CSR version (because it requires request encryption). We can detect a HAL that supports old CSRs by checking which interfaces it supports.\n\nHowever, a newer HAL doesn\u0027t _have_ to support the old CSR version. In that case, CURVE_NONE is the supported curve since that HAL will never support CSR encryption.\n\nAs usual, we made something complicated. Feel free to grab me over chat or gvc if you want to talk more.",
      "parentUuid": "5cca3fb6_8b13c57c",
      "revId": "3730df0e0d2f0cb2e4b27199615abfdff16b9701",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c43a3a7_0d5d0c63",
        "filename": "security/keymint/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 224,
      "author": {
        "id": 1678423
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-07T00:41:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks a lot Seth, for the clarifications and for offering to chat over gvc. I added a calendar event for tomorrow evening.\n\nFollowing are the few followup questions I have. If you think it is easier to clarify them in the meeting, no need to reply here.\n\n\u003e allow v3 implementations to support v2 clients\n\nI am curious what would be an example of a scenario where the IRPC implementation is in V3, but the clients are in V2?\n\nThat also raises the question who are the clients of an IRPC implementation? Are they the keystore and the remote provisioner? Or the remote provisioner only? Or are there any other clients that I am not aware of?\n\n(If the clients are only on the Android side and since Android is upgraded with each firmware upgrades (although it is not the case the other way round) I am curious how could a V3 implementation have a v2 client. I am sure that I must be missing something)\n\n\u003e The confusing bit here, I think, is that there are two different versions:\nThe HAL version\nThe CSR version (the on-the-wire format that the HAL spits out)\n\nI hope that by CSR version, you meant the version here?\nhttps://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:hardware/interfaces/security/rkp/aidl/android/hardware/security/keymint/IRemotelyProvisionedComponent.aidl;l\u003d319\n\nYes, I thought that both those are the same for an IRPC V3 implementation.\n\nAnd since there is no explicit CSR version field in IRPC V2 messages, I assume that it is inferred by checking whether the HAL implements the generateCSRRequest method or not.\n\nThanks again!",
      "parentUuid": "35c0e410_2cfd477f",
      "revId": "3730df0e0d2f0cb2e4b27199615abfdff16b9701",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
